[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...e5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/32332d47-f014-4189-b333-4a5399b1d683/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. This is the starting position of the Sicilian Defense, which is one of the most complex openings in all of chess. Simply put, the move c5 introduces asymmetry into the position, and its primary purpose is to control the important d4 square. In this course, we're going to play b3, intending a fianchetto. This is a more positional approach which aims to take our opponents into non-theoretical waters that they are probably unfamiliar with.} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. Black continues natural development and increases their control over the center. We follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...e5 {Black plays e5, taking central space and increasing their control over the d4 square. Against Nc6 and e5, we'll form a light-square complex by developing our light-squared bishop to c4.} 4.Bc4 4...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight, immediately attacking the e4-pawn. We'll defend the e-pawn by developing our queenside knight to c3.} 5.Nc3 5...Be7 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop to e7, preparing O-O. Black could also play d6, but it would soon transpose. Now that our light-squared bishop has been developed, we'll play d3 and establish a solid light-squared pawn chain.} 6.d3 6...O-O {Black castles their king. We'll develop our kingside knight to e2. This is important since we want to castle and push f4.} 7.Nge2 7...d6 {Black plays d6, supporting the central pawns and opening up the light-squared bishop. We'll castle our king.} 8.O-O 8...a6 {Black plays a6, looking to expand on the queenside with b5. We'll play a4, preventing Black from playing b5. This is how we want to be meeting any intended expansion with a6.} 9.a4 9...Be6 {Black develops the bishop to e6, looking to trade light-squared bishops.} 10.f4 {We expand with f4. With the dominant control over the light-squares and great attacking prospects on the kingside, White has an edge and should have a relatively easy game. If Black ever captures our c4 bishop, we should recapture with the b-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...e5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/32332d47-f014-4189-b333-4a5399b1d683/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. This is the starting position of the Sicilian Defense, which is one of the most complex openings in all of chess. Simply put, the move c5 introduces asymmetry into the position, and its primary purpose is to control the important d4 square. In this course, we're going to play b3, intending a fianchetto. This is a more positional approach which aims to take our opponents into non-theoretical waters that they are probably unfamiliar with.} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. Black continues natural development and increases their control over the center. We follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...e5 {Black plays e5, taking central space and increasing their control over the d4 square. Against Nc6 and e5, we'll form a light-square complex by developing our light-squared bishop to c4.} 4.Bc4 4...d6 {Black plays d6, supporting the central pawns and opening up the light-squared bishop. We'll strike with f4, looking to break apart Black's solid central pawn formation!} 5.f4 5...exf4 {Black captures the "free" pawn on f4. We'll move our queen out to f3, looking to recapture the pawn on f4.} 6.Qf3 6...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight. We'll capture the pawn on f4. Instead of ...Nf6, the scary-looking Qh4+ could easily be met with g3.} 7.Qxf4 7...Be7 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop, preparing O-O. We'll develop our kingside knight to f3.} 8.Nf3 8...O-O {Black castles their king.} 9.Nc3 {We develop our other knight to c3. With the better piece quality & activity, White certainly has a slight edge. We can look to castle queenside and solidify our pawn structure with d3.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...e5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/32332d47-f014-4189-b333-4a5399b1d683/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. This is the starting position of the Sicilian Defense, which is one of the most complex openings in all of chess. Simply put, the move c5 introduces asymmetry into the position, and its primary purpose is to control the important d4 square. In this course, we're going to play b3, intending a fianchetto. This is a more positional approach which aims to take our opponents into non-theoretical waters that they are probably unfamiliar with.} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. Black continues natural development and increases their control over the center. We follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...e5 {Black plays e5, taking central space and increasing their control over the d4 square. Against Nc6 and e5, we'll form a light-square complex by developing our light-squared bishop to c4.} 4.Bc4 4...d6 {Black plays d6, supporting the central pawns and opening up the light-squared bishop. We'll strike with f4, looking to break apart Black's solid central pawn formation!} 5.f4 5...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight. We'll develop our kingside knight, adding an attacker to the e5-pawn and preparing to castle kingside. If Black now captures on f4, we should jump our knight into g5, hitting f7!} 6.Nf3 6...Be7 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop, preparing O-O. We'll capture the pawn on e5. If Black now recaptures on e5, we can capture once more with our knight.} 7.fxe5 7...Nxe4 {Black captures the pawn on e4. We'll move our bishop to d5, attacking the e4 knight and threatening to double Black's c-pawns.} 8.Bd5 8...Ng5 {Black moves the knight to the only safe square, g5. We'll capture on c6, giving up the bishop pair, but doubling Black's pawns.} 9.Bxc6+ 9...bxc6 {Black recaptures on c6.} 10.exd6 {We capture the pawn on d6. With Black's ruined structure and a great bishop on b2, White has a pleasant advantage. If Black recaptures with the bishop, we can continue Qe2+, Nc3 and O-O-O. And if Black recaptures with the queen, we can continue Nxg5, Qe2+ and Bxg7.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...e5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/32332d47-f014-4189-b333-4a5399b1d683/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. This is the starting position of the Sicilian Defense, which is one of the most complex openings in all of chess. Simply put, the move c5 introduces asymmetry into the position, and its primary purpose is to control the important d4 square. In this course, we're going to play b3, intending a fianchetto. This is a more positional approach which aims to take our opponents into non-theoretical waters that they are probably unfamiliar with.} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. Black continues natural development and increases their control over the center. We follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...e5 {Black plays e5, taking central space and increasing their control over the d4 square. Against Nc6 and e5, we'll form a light-square complex by developing our light-squared bishop to c4.} 4.Bc4 4...d6 {Black plays d6, supporting the central pawns and opening up the light-squared bishop. We'll strike with f4, looking to break apart Black's solid central pawn formation!} 5.f4 5...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight. We'll develop our kingside knight, adding an attacker to the e5-pawn and preparing to castle kingside. If Black now captures on f4, we should jump our knight into g5, hitting f7!} 6.Nf3 6...Nxe4 {Black captures the pawn on e4. We'll move our bishop to d5, attacking the e4 knight and threatening to double Black's c-pawns.} 7.Bd5 7...Nf6 {Black moves the knight to the only safe square, f6. We'll capture on c6, giving up the bishop pair, but doubling Black's pawns.} 8.Bxc6+ 8...bxc6 {Black recaptures on c6. We'll capture the pawn on e5.} 9.fxe5 9...dxe5 {Black recaptures on e5.} 10.Nxe5 {We capture on e5 with the knight. Material is now balanced, and, with Black's awful pawn structure on the queenside, White should have a comfortable long-term edge. We will continue developing with O-O and Na3-Nc4.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...e5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/32332d47-f014-4189-b333-4a5399b1d683/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. This is the starting position of the Sicilian Defense, which is one of the most complex openings in all of chess. Simply put, the move c5 introduces asymmetry into the position, and its primary purpose is to control the important d4 square. In this course, we're going to play b3, intending a fianchetto. This is a more positional approach which aims to take our opponents into non-theoretical waters that they are probably unfamiliar with.} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. Black continues natural development and increases their control over the center. We follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...e5 {Black plays e5, taking central space and increasing their control over the d4 square. Against Nc6 and e5, we'll form a light-square complex by developing our light-squared bishop to c4.} 4.Bc4 4...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight, immediately attacking the e4-pawn. We'll defend the e-pawn by developing our queenside knight to c3.} 5.Nc3 5...Nxe4 {Black also has the neat tactical idea Nxe4 for us to consider. We'll capture the knight on e4.} 6.Nxe4 6...d5 {Black strikes with d5, forking our knight and bishop. We'll retreat the bishop to d3.} 7.Bd3 7...dxe4 {Black captures our knight, regaining the sacrificed piece.} 8.Bxe4 {We capture the pawn on e4. The computer evaluates the position as equal - Black has the space advantage, but we have the more active pieces and a lead in development. We can look to continue developing with Nf3 and O-O, while the immediate threat is now Bxc6+, doubling Black's c-pawns.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...e5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/32332d47-f014-4189-b333-4a5399b1d683/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. This is the starting position of the Sicilian Defense, which is one of the most complex openings in all of chess. Simply put, the move c5 introduces asymmetry into the position, and its primary purpose is to control the important d4 square. In this course, we're going to play b3, intending a fianchetto. This is a more positional approach which aims to take our opponents into non-theoretical waters that they are probably unfamiliar with.} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. Black continues natural development and increases their control over the center. We follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...e5 {Black plays e5, taking central space and increasing their control over the d4 square. Against Nc6 and e5, we'll form a light-square complex by developing our light-squared bishop to c4.} 4.Bc4 4...d6 {Black plays d6, supporting the central pawns and opening up the light-squared bishop. We'll strike with f4, looking to break apart Black's solid central pawn formation!} 5.f4 5...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight. We'll develop our kingside knight, adding an attacker to the e5-pawn and preparing to castle kingside. If Black now captures on f4, we should jump our knight into g5, hitting f7!} 6.Nf3 6...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop and pins our knight. We'll capture the pawn on e5.} 7.fxe5 7...dxe5 {Black recaptures with the d-pawn. We'll target the bishop with h3.} 8.h3 8...Bh5 {If Black captures on f3, we should simply recapture with the queen, and any Nd4 can be met with Bxd4. Instead, Black maintains the pin by retreating the bishop to h5. We'll attack the bishop once more with g4!} 9.g4 9...Bg6 {Black retreats the bishop to the only safe square.} 10.d3 {We support the e-pawn with d3. White now has a comfortable advantage and promising attacking prospects on the kingside. After Black deals with the threat on their e-pawn, we can continue to develop with Nc3 and O-O. Also, a4 is always a useful move which looks to prevent Black from expanding on the queenside (with a6 and b5).} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 1: Black Plays 3...e5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/32332d47-f014-4189-b333-4a5399b1d683/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. This is the starting position of the Sicilian Defense, which is one of the most complex openings in all of chess. Simply put, the move c5 introduces asymmetry into the position, and its primary purpose is to control the important d4 square. In this course, we're going to play b3, intending a fianchetto. This is a more positional approach which aims to take our opponents into non-theoretical waters that they are probably unfamiliar with.} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. Black continues natural development and increases their control over the center. We follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...e5 {Black plays e5, taking central space and increasing their control over the d4 square. Against Nc6 and e5, we'll form a light-square complex by developing our light-squared bishop to c4.} 4.Bc4 4...d6 {Black plays d6, supporting the central pawns and opening up the light-squared bishop. We'll strike with f4, looking to break apart Black's solid central pawn formation!} 5.f4 5...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight. We'll develop our kingside knight, adding an attacker to the e5-pawn and preparing to castle kingside. If Black now captures on f4, we should jump our knight into g5, hitting f7!} 6.Nf3 6...Bg4 {Black develops the light-squared bishop and pins our knight. We'll capture the pawn on e5.} 7.fxe5 7...Nxe5 {Black recaptures with the knight. This looks natural, but is a huge mistake! We'll capture on e5. Giving up the strong dark-squared bishop might seem odd at first glance, but there is a tactical reason behind this!} 8.Bxe5 8...dxe5 {Black recaptures on e5. Boom! We'll sacrifice our bishop on f7!} 9.Bxf7+ 9...Kxf7 {Black captures on f7.} 10.Nxe5+ {We capture on e5, forking Black's king & bishop and regaining our sacrificed piece. With the two extra pawns and Black's weak king, White now simply has a winning advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 2: Black Plays 3...d6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/d90ccf83-0c7e-4975-ab5a-aa4fab010d26/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Now for our 2.b3 Sicilian! We will fianchetto our bishop.} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. Black continues natural development and increases their control over the center. We follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...d6 {Black plays d6, supporting the c5-pawn and opening up the light-squared bishop. Against this, we'll pin Black's knight with Bb5 and look to set up a d3/e4/f4 pawn structure. Black now has three options...} 4.Bb5 4...e5 {Black might also play e5, taking central space and increasing control over the d4 square. We'll develop our kingside knight to e2 as to keep the f4 expansion available.} 5.Ne2 5...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e-pawn. We'll support the e-pawn with d3.} 6.d3 6...Be7 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop, preparing O-O. We'll castle our king.} 7.O-O 7...O-O {Black castles their king. We'll capture on c6, giving up the bishop pair, but doubling Black's pawns.} 8.Bxc6 8...bxc6 {Black recaptures on c6.} 9.Ng3 {We move our knight to g3. White now has a slight edge, and we'll look to continue with Nd2 and f4.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 2: Black Plays 3...d6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/d90ccf83-0c7e-4975-ab5a-aa4fab010d26/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Now for our 2.b3 Sicilian! We will fianchetto our bishop.} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. Black continues natural development and increases their control over the center. We follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...d6 {Black plays d6, supporting the c5-pawn and opening up the light-squared bishop. Against this, we'll pin Black's knight with Bb5 and look to set up a d3/e4/f4 pawn structure. Black now has three options...} 4.Bb5 4...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight, immediately attacking the e4-pawn. We'll defend the e-pawn by developing our queenside knight to c3.} 5.Nc3 5...g6 {Black might also look to develop in "Dragon" style with g6 and Bg7. We'll sacrifice a pawn with e5!} 6.e5 6...dxe5 {Black captures on e5. We capture on c6, giving up the bishop pair, but doubling Black's pawns.} 7.Bxc6+ 7...bxc6 {Black recaptures on c6.} 8.Qe2 {We move our queen to e2 and attack the e-pawn. The evaluation of this position lingers around equality, but with Black's awful structure White's chances shouldn't be underestimated. We are bound to win the pawn back, and we'll continue to develop with Nf3 and O-O-O.} *

