1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Now for our 2.b3 Sicilian! We will fianchetto our bishop.} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. Black continues natural development and increases their control over the center. We follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...d6 {Black plays d6, supporting the c5-pawn and opening up the light-squared bishop. Against this, we'll pin Black's knight with Bb5 and look to set up a d3/e4/f4 pawn structure. Black now has three options...} 4.Bb5 4...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight, immediately attacking the e4-pawn. We'll defend the e-pawn by developing our queenside knight to c3.} 5.Nc3 5...Bd7 {Black unpins the knight by developing the light-squared bishop to d7.} 6.f4 {We expand with f4, transposing back into the main line.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 2: Black Plays 3...d6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/d90ccf83-0c7e-4975-ab5a-aa4fab010d26/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Now for our 2.b3 Sicilian! We will fianchetto our bishop.} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. Black continues natural development and increases their control over the center. We follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...d6 {Black plays d6, supporting the c5-pawn and opening up the light-squared bishop. Against this, we'll pin Black's knight with Bb5 and look to set up a d3/e4/f4 pawn structure. Black now has three options...} 4.Bb5 4...Bd7 {Black unpins the knight by developing the light-squared bishop to d7.} 5.f4 {We expand with f4. The computer gives equality, but White has more space, and the position just seems easier to play for White. We'll continue by developing our knights (Nc3 and Nf3), and playing Qe2 and O-O-O. To gain a better understanding of these positions, check out the game Carlsen - Ehlvest, 2018. a6 should be met with Bxc6.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 2: Black Plays 3...d6 / Variation 5"]
