1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. Now for our 2.b3 Sicilian! We will fianchetto our bishop.} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. Black continues natural development and increases their control over the center. We follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...d6 {Black plays d6, supporting the c5-pawn and opening up the light-squared bishop. Against this, we'll pin Black's knight with Bb5 and look to set up a d3/e4/f4 pawn structure. Black now has three options...} 4.Bb5 4...e5 {Black might also play e5, taking central space and increasing control over the d4 square. We'll develop our kingside knight to e2 as to keep the f4 expansion available.} 5.Ne2 5...Bd7 {Black unpins the knight by developing the light-squared bishop to d7.} 6.d3 {Now that our light-squared bishop has been developed, we play d3 and establish a solid light-squared pawn chain. White now has a comfortable advantage, and we'll look to continue with O-O, Nc3/Nd2 and f4.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 3: b3 Sicilian with 2...Nc6 (3...e6) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/ccecfc90-124d-48f1-91cb-1bfe9f2dbbc1/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll play our 2.b3 Sicilian and fianchetto our bishop!} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1- bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...e6 {Quite often, Black plays e6 on move three. By doing so, Black opens up the dark-squared bishop and is preparing a future central expansion with d5. Black's setup is flexible but now I recommend Bb5. This is a sneaky idea with the same intentions as before: trade the bishop, play d3 and f4 etc.} 4.Bb5 4...Nge7 {Black develops the kingside knight to e7, supporting the c6 knight. We'll develop our kingside knight to f3, preparing O-O.} 5.Nf3 5...a6 {Black attacks the bishop with a6. We'll capture on c6 - we're giving up the bishop pair, but looking to obtain a considerable lead in development. Black has made a lot of pawn moves in the opening.} 6.Bxc6 6...Nxc6 {Black recaptures with the knight as to not ruin their pawn structure. We'll now castle.} 7.O-O 7...d5 {Black can also choose to strike immediately in the center with d5. We'll proceed by capturing on d5.} 8.exd5 8...Qxd5 {Black recaptures with the queen. If Black takes with the pawn, we can respond with d4 and follow it up with Nc3. We'll develop our knight to c3 and attack Black's queen.} 9.Nc3 9...Qd8 {Black retreats the queen to its home square, d8. A more active move like ...Qh5 would have run into difficulties after Na4-Nb6.} 10.Ne4 {We jump our knight into e4. This move prevents Black from developing the dark-squared bishop, since it is tied down to the defense of the g7-pawn. White now has a comfortable advantage, and we can look to strike in the center with d4.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 3: b3 Sicilian with 2...Nc6 (3...e6) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/ccecfc90-124d-48f1-91cb-1bfe9f2dbbc1/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll play our 2.b3 Sicilian and fianchetto our bishop!} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1- bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...e6 {Quite often, Black plays e6 on move three. By doing so, Black opens up the dark-squared bishop and is preparing a future central expansion with d5. Black's setup is flexible but now I recommend Bb5. This is a sneaky idea with the same intentions as before: trade the bishop, play d3 and f4 etc.} 4.Bb5 4...Nge7 {Black develops the kingside knight to e7, supporting the c6 knight. We'll develop our kingside knight to f3, preparing O-O.} 5.Nf3 5...a6 {Black attacks the bishop with a6. We'll capture on c6 - we're giving up the bishop pair, but looking to obtain a considerable lead in development. Black has made a lot of pawn moves in the opening.} 6.Bxc6 6...Nxc6 {Black recaptures with the knight as to not ruin their pawn structure. We'll now castle.} 7.O-O 7...d6 {Most often, Black plays d6. Against this, we'll immediately strike in the center with d4.} 8.d4 8...cxd4 {Black captures the pawn on d4. We'll recapture with the knight.} 9.Nxd4 9...e5 {Black attacks the knight with e5. We'll capture the knight on c6.} 10.Nxc6 10...bxc6 {Black recaptures on c6. We'll continue with c4, controlling the important d5 square.} 11.c4 11...Be7 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop, preparing O-O. Now we'll strike with c5, looking to sacrifice a pawn and damage Black's structure beyond repair.} 12.c5 12...O-O {Black castles the king. We'll capture on d6.} 13.cxd6 13...Bxd6 {Black recaptures with the bishop. If Black recaptures with the queen, we should trade on d6 and enter a pleasant endgame where we will try to expose Black's weak queenside pawns.} 14.Nd2 {We develop our knight to d2, looking to jump it into c4. With Black's weak queenside pawns, White now has a very comfortable edge and should have a relatively easy game.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 3: b3 Sicilian with 2...Nc6 (3...e6) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/ccecfc90-124d-48f1-91cb-1bfe9f2dbbc1/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll play our 2.b3 Sicilian and fianchetto our bishop!} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1- bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...e6 {Quite often, Black plays e6 on move three. By doing so, Black opens up the dark-squared bishop and is preparing a future central expansion with d5. Black's setup is flexible but now I recommend Bb5. This is a sneaky idea with the same intentions as before: trade the bishop, play d3 and f4 etc.} 4.Bb5 4...Nge7 {Black develops the kingside knight to e7, supporting the c6 knight. We'll develop our kingside knight to f3, preparing O-O.} 5.Nf3 5...a6 {Black attacks the bishop with a6. We'll capture on c6 - we're giving up the bishop pair, but looking to obtain a considerable lead in development. Black has made a lot of pawn moves in the opening.} 6.Bxc6 6...Nxc6 {Black recaptures with the knight as to not ruin their pawn structure. We'll now castle.} 7.O-O 7...f6 {Some players go for f6, looking to negate the effect of our powerful bishop on b2. Against this, we'll immediately strike in the center with d4.} 8.d4 8...cxd4 {Black captures the pawn on d4. We'll recapture with the knight.} 9.Nxd4 9...Be7 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop, looking to castle their king. Not so fast! We'll move our queen out to h5 and give a check to Black's king.} 10.Qh5+ 10...g6 {Black blocks the check with g6.} 11.Qh6 {We slide our queen into h6, preventing Black from castling. White is now certainly better and it's not so easy for Black to survive. It is hard to suggest a general plan, but some common attacking ideas include Nxc6 and e5, and even the simple Qg7. The retreating move Bf8 can simply be met with Qe3.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 3: b3 Sicilian with 2...Nc6 (3...e6) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/ccecfc90-124d-48f1-91cb-1bfe9f2dbbc1/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll play our 2.b3 Sicilian and fianchetto our bishop!} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1- bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...e6 {Quite often, Black plays e6 on move three. By doing so, Black opens up the dark-squared bishop and is preparing a future central expansion with d5. Black's setup is flexible but now I recommend Bb5. This is a sneaky idea with the same intentions as before: trade the bishop, play d3 and f4 etc.} 4.Bb5 4...Nge7 {Black develops the kingside knight to e7, supporting the c6 knight. We'll develop our kingside knight to f3, preparing O-O.} 5.Nf3 5...a6 {Black attacks the bishop with a6. We'll capture on c6 - we're giving up the bishop pair, but looking to obtain a considerable lead in development. Black has made a lot of pawn moves in the opening.} 6.Bxc6 6...Nxc6 {Black recaptures with the knight as to not ruin their pawn structure. We'll now castle.} 7.O-O 7...d6 {Most often, Black plays d6. Against this, we'll immediately strike in the center with d4.} 8.d4 8...cxd4 {Black captures the pawn on d4. We'll recapture with the knight.} 9.Nxd4 9...Bd7 {Black supports the c6 knight by developing the bishop to d7.} 10.c4 {We continue with c4. The arising pawn structure is known as the "Maróczy Bind" - White's c and e-pawns control the d5-square, making it difficult for Black to expand with ...d5. White has a slight objective edge. We'll look to continue developing with Nc3, Qd2 and Rfe1.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 3: b3 Sicilian with 2...Nc6 (3...e6) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/ccecfc90-124d-48f1-91cb-1bfe9f2dbbc1/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll play our 2.b3 Sicilian and fianchetto our bishop!} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1- bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...e6 {Quite often, Black plays e6 on move three. By doing so, Black opens up the dark-squared bishop and is preparing a future central expansion with d5. Black's setup is flexible but now I recommend Bb5. This is a sneaky idea with the same intentions as before: trade the bishop, play d3 and f4 etc.} 4.Bb5 4...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight, immediately attacking the e4-pawn. We'll capture on c6, giving up the bishop pair, but doubling Black's pawns.} 5.Bxc6 5...bxc6 {Black recaptures with the b-pawn. We'll march forward and attack Black's knight with e5!} 6.e5 6...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5.} 7.d3 {We continue d3. White now has a comfortable edge - the e5-pawn is a permanent thorn in Black's side and the doubled c-pawns are a liability. We can kick out Black's knight with c4, and continue developing with Ne2, Nd2 and O-O. If Black tries to get rid of our e-pawn with d6, we should kick out Black's knight and follow up by supporting our e-pawn with f4.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 3: b3 Sicilian with 2...Nc6 (3...e6) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/ccecfc90-124d-48f1-91cb-1bfe9f2dbbc1/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll play our 2.b3 Sicilian and fianchetto our bishop!} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1- bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...e6 {Quite often, Black plays e6 on move three. By doing so, Black opens up the dark-squared bishop and is preparing a future central expansion with d5. Black's setup is flexible but now I recommend Bb5. This is a sneaky idea with the same intentions as before: trade the bishop, play d3 and f4 etc.} 4.Bb5 4...Nd4 {Black jumps the knight into d4 and attacks the b5-bishop. This isn't super common, but it is one of the critical ways for Black to fight against the b5-bishop. We'll retreat the bishop to d3. It looks weird - but Black can't take advantage of it.} 5.Bd3 5...d5 {Black strikes in the center with d5. Nf6 is also possible, but, as you'll see, Nf3 is a natural and good response in this circumstance. We'll develop our queenside knight.} 6.Nc3 6...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight. We'll develop our other knight, challenging the knight outpost on d4.} 7.Nf3 7...dxe4 {Black releases the tension by capturing on e4. This is natural, but inaccurate.} 8.Nxe4 {We recapture on e4 with the knight. White has the more active pieces and a considerable lead in development. Play can develop in a handful of ways, but a common plan is Nxd4, followed by Qf3 and O-O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 3: b3 Sicilian with 2...Nc6 (3...e6) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/ccecfc90-124d-48f1-91cb-1bfe9f2dbbc1/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll play our 2.b3 Sicilian and fianchetto our bishop!} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1- bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...e6 {Quite often, Black plays e6 on move three. By doing so, Black opens up the dark-squared bishop and is preparing a future central expansion with d5. Black's setup is flexible but now I recommend Bb5. This is a sneaky idea with the same intentions as before: trade the bishop, play d3 and f4 etc.} 4.Bb5 4...Nd4 {Black jumps the knight into d4 and attacks the b5-bishop. This isn't super common, but it is one of the critical ways for Black to fight against the b5-bishop. We'll retreat the bishop to d3. It looks weird - but Black can't take advantage of it.} 5.Bd3 5...d5 {Black strikes in the center with d5. Nf6 is also possible, but, as you'll see, Nf3 is a natural and good response in this circumstance. We'll develop our queenside knight.} 6.Nc3 6...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight. We'll develop our other knight, challenging the knight outpost on d4.} 7.Nf3 7...Nxf3+ {Black captures the knight on f3. We'll recapture with the queen. We have a clear lead in development, and after all, Black has now moved their queen's knight three times in the opening.} 8.Qxf3 8...d4 {Black plays d4, attacking our knight and closing the long diagonal.} 9.Ne2 {We retreat the knight to e2. The evaluation lingers around equality, but Black is the one who has to play accurately. We will look to break with c3/b4 and castle kingside. If Black tries to bolster the d-pawn with e5, we can look to play Qg3 and f4.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 3: b3 Sicilian with 2...Nc6 (3...e6) / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/ccecfc90-124d-48f1-91cb-1bfe9f2dbbc1/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll play our 2.b3 Sicilian and fianchetto our bishop!} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1- bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...e6 {Quite often, Black plays e6 on move three. By doing so, Black opens up the dark-squared bishop and is preparing a future central expansion with d5. Black's setup is flexible but now I recommend Bb5. This is a sneaky idea with the same intentions as before: trade the bishop, play d3 and f4 etc.} 4.Bb5 4...d5 {Black immediately strikes in the center with d5. We'll capture on c6, giving up the bishop pair, but doubling Black's pawns.} 5.Bxc6+ 5...bxc6 {Black recaptures on c6.} 6.d3 {We support the e-pawn with d3. There is no doubt that White is now slightly better - Black has the inferior structure and our light-squared pawn chain is negating the potential impact of Black's c8 bishop. We'll look to continue developing with Nd2, Nf3, Qe2 and O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 4: b3 Sicilian with 2...Nc6 (3...Nf6) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/9525d157-a1c5-42cc-b103-4f0335008823/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll play our b3 Sicilian and prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e-pawn. We'll push forward and attack Black's knight.} 4.e5 4...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. We'll develop our knight to f3. This leaves Black with a few choices for us to examine.} 5.Nf3 5...e6 {Most common is e6: Black supports the d5-knight and prepares to develop the dark-squared bishop. Against e6 we'll look to develop with g3, Bg2 and O-O.} 6.g3 6...Be7 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop and prepares O-O. We'll continue with our plan by fianchettoing the light-squared bishop.} 7.Bg2 7...O-O {Black castles their king. We will do likewise.} 8.O-O 8...f6 {Black can also try to get rid of our e5-pawn with f6. We'll attack Black's knight with c4.} 9.c4 9...Nc7 {Most often, Black retreats the knight back to c7. We'll expand in the center with d4!} 10.d4 10...cxd4 {Black captures the pawn on d4. Before recapturing on d4, it is important that we throw in the capture on f6.} 11.exf6 11...Bxf6 {Black recaptures with the bishop. We'll capture on d4 with the knight.} 12.Nxd4 12...d5 {Black strikes in the center with d5. We'll capture the knight on c6.} 13.Nxc6 13...bxc6 {Black recaptures on c6 - this is of course forced, since the queen was attacked. We'll trade dark-squared bishops on f6.} 14.Bxf6 14...Qxf6 {Black recaptures with the queen.} 15.Nd2 {We develop our knight to d2. With the better structure (two pawn islands vs Black's three) and more active minor pieces, White now has a comfortable advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 4: b3 Sicilian with 2...Nc6 (3...Nf6) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/9525d157-a1c5-42cc-b103-4f0335008823/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll play our b3 Sicilian and prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e-pawn. We'll push forward and attack Black's knight.} 4.e5 4...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. We'll develop our knight to f3. This leaves Black with a few choices for us to examine.} 5.Nf3 5...e6 {Most common is e6: Black supports the d5-knight and prepares to develop the dark-squared bishop. Against e6 we'll look to develop with g3, Bg2 and O-O.} 6.g3 6...Be7 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop and prepares O-O. We'll continue with our plan by fianchettoing the light-squared bishop.} 7.Bg2 7...O-O {Black castles their king. We will do likewise.} 8.O-O 8...f6 {Black can also try to get rid of our e5-pawn with f6. We'll attack Black's knight with c4.} 9.c4 9...Ndb4 {Black opts to jump the knight into b4. We'll capture the pawn on f6.} 10.exf6 10...Bxf6 {Black recaptures with the bishop. We'll trade dark-squared bishops on f6.} 11.Bxf6 11...Qxf6 {Black recaptures with the queen.} 12.Nc3 {We develop our knight to c3. White is, again, better - our pieces are more active and better placed, and Black's bishop is having an existential crisis. We'll look to kick Black's knight away with a3 and can try to jump our knight into e4.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 4: b3 Sicilian with 2...Nc6 (3...Nf6) / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/9525d157-a1c5-42cc-b103-4f0335008823/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll play our b3 Sicilian and prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e-pawn. We'll push forward and attack Black's knight.} 4.e5 4...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. We'll develop our knight to f3. This leaves Black with a few choices for us to examine.} 5.Nf3 5...g6 {Black can also play g6, intending to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop. Against this, we'll develop our bishop to b5, looking to eventually capture the knight and double Black's c-pawns.} 6.Bb5 6...Bg7 {Black continues with their plan and fianchettoes the f8-bishop. We'll proceed by castling.} 7.O-O 7...O-O {Black also castles. We'll move our rook to e1, adding some support to the e5-pawn.} 8.Re1 8...d6 {Black's other option is d6, looking to immediately get rid of our e-pawn. We'll capture on c6 and force Black to double their c-pawns.} 9.Bxc6 9...bxc6 {Black recaptures on c6.} 10.d3 {We continue with d3. Due to Black's inferior structure, White has a slight, but lasting positional edge. We can look to continue with Nbd2. A future Bg4 can and should be met with h3.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 4: b3 Sicilian with 2...Nc6 (3...Nf6) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/9525d157-a1c5-42cc-b103-4f0335008823/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll play our b3 Sicilian and prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e-pawn. We'll push forward and attack Black's knight.} 4.e5 4...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. We'll develop our knight to f3. This leaves Black with a few choices for us to examine.} 5.Nf3 5...e6 {Most common is e6: Black supports the d5-knight and prepares to develop the dark-squared bishop. Against e6 we'll look to develop with g3, Bg2 and O-O.} 6.g3 6...Be7 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop and prepares O-O. We'll continue with our plan by fianchettoing the light-squared bishop.} 7.Bg2 7...O-O {Black castles their king. We will do likewise.} 8.O-O 8...d6 {The most common move here is d6. Black is looking to get rid of our dominating e-pawn. We prefer it if Black captures on e5, so we'll simply proceed by moving our rook over to the e-file.} 9.Re1 9...dxe5 {Black captures the pawn on e5. We'll recapture on e5 with the knight.} 10.Nxe5 10...Nxe5 {Black trades knights on e5. We'll recapture with the bishop.} 11.Bxe5 11...Bf6 {Black moves the bishop to f6, looking to trade off our powerful dark-squared bishop.} 12.d4 {We support the bishop and expand in the center with d4. White is now comfortably better - our pieces are very active, the g2-bishop is a menace, and Black's c8 bishop won't be impressing anyone anytime soon. If Black now captures on e5, we should recapture with the d-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 4: b3 Sicilian with 2...Nc6 (3...Nf6) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/9525d157-a1c5-42cc-b103-4f0335008823/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll play our b3 Sicilian and prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e-pawn. We'll push forward and attack Black's knight.} 4.e5 4...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. We'll develop our knight to f3. This leaves Black with a few choices for us to examine.} 5.Nf3 5...g6 {Black can also play g6, intending to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop. Against this, we'll develop our bishop to b5, looking to eventually capture the knight and double Black's c-pawns.} 6.Bb5 6...Bg7 {Black continues with their plan and fianchettoes the f8-bishop. We'll proceed by castling.} 7.O-O 7...O-O {Black also castles. We'll move our rook to e1, adding some support to the e5-pawn.} 8.Re1 8...Nc7 {Most often, Black voluntarily retreats the knight and attacks the b5-bishop. Now that we've been provoked, we'll capture on c6 and force Black to double their c-pawns.} 9.Bxc6 9...dxc6 {Black recaptures with the d-pawn.} 10.d3 {We continue d3. Due to Black's inferior structure, White has a slight, but lasting, positional edge. We should look to continue with Nbd2 and a4. Any future Bg4 from Black can and should be met with h3.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 4: b3 Sicilian with 2...Nc6 (3...Nf6) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/9525d157-a1c5-42cc-b103-4f0335008823/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll play our b3 Sicilian and prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e-pawn. We'll push forward and attack Black's knight.} 4.e5 4...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. We'll develop our knight to f3. This leaves Black with a few choices for us to examine.} 5.Nf3 5...d6 {A close second in popularity is d6. Black creates tension between the central pawns and opens up the c8 bishop. Against this, we'll develop our light-squared bishop out to b5, pinning the c6-knight and looking to double Black's c-pawns.} 6.Bb5 6...Bd7 {Most often, Black unpins the knight by developing the c8-bishop to d7. We'll proceed by capturing on d6.} 7.exd6 7...exd6 {Black recaptures on d6. We'll castle our king, perhaps looking to bring a rook to the e-file.} 8.O-O 8...Be7 {Black prepares to castle kingside by developing the dark-squared bishop. At first glance, it looks like this move hangs the g7-pawn, but capturing it is simply too risky and would give Black great counterplay on the kingside. Instead, we'll strike in the center with d4!} 9.d4 9...cxd4 {Black captures the pawn on d4. We'll recapture with the knight. If instead of this Black voluntarily retreats the knight to c7, attacking our b5-bishop, we can respond with c4.} 10.Nxd4 10...O-O {Black castles their king.} 11.c4 {We attack Black's knight and expand with c4. With the more active pieces and better structure, White now has a comfortable edge. After Black moves the knight, we'll look to capture on c6 (with the bishop) and develop our knight to c3. Our f1-rook will develop to e1, and our queen can often come out to f3.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 4: b3 Sicilian with 2...Nc6 (3...Nf6) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/9525d157-a1c5-42cc-b103-4f0335008823/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll play our b3 Sicilian and prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e-pawn. We'll push forward and attack Black's knight.} 4.e5 4...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. We'll develop our knight to f3. This leaves Black with a few choices for us to examine.} 5.Nf3 5...d6 {A close second in popularity is d6. Black creates tension between the central pawns and opens up the c8 bishop. Against this, we'll develop our light-squared bishop out to b5, pinning the c6-knight and looking to double Black's c-pawns.} 6.Bb5 6...Bd7 {Most often, Black unpins the knight by developing the c8-bishop to d7. We'll proceed by capturing on d6.} 7.exd6 7...e6 {Black also has the interesting option of e6, looking to recapture on d6 with the f8-bishop. Against this, we'll develop our queenside knight to c3.} 8.Nc3 8...Bxd6 {Black captures the pawn on d6. We'll capture Black's knight on d5.} 9.Nxd5 9...exd5 {Black recaptures on d5. We'll move our queen to e2 and give a check to Black's king.} 10.Qe2+ 10...Qe7 {Black blocks the check with Qe7, offering a trade of queens.} 11.d4 {We expand in the center with d4. After best play from both sides, the evaluation is close to equality, with a slight edge for White. We'll castle queenside, and should look to capture on c5 in order to isolate Black's d-pawn. If Black now trades queens on e2, we should recapture with the bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Black Plays 2...Nc6 / Study 4: b3 Sicilian with 2...Nc6 (3...Nf6) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/b3d4a50a-1de3-4e8c-9889-01a6256699e7/studies/9525d157-a1c5-42cc-b103-4f0335008823/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll play our b3 Sicilian and prepare to fianchetto our bishop.} 2.b3 2...Nc6 {The main continuation is 2...Nc6. We follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks the e-pawn. We'll push forward and attack Black's knight.} 4.e5 4...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. We'll develop our knight to f3. This leaves Black with a few choices for us to examine.} 5.Nf3 5...d6 {A close second in popularity is d6. Black creates tension between the central pawns and opens up the c8 bishop. Against this, we'll develop our light-squared bishop out to b5, pinning the c6-knight and looking to double Black's c-pawns.} 6.Bb5 6...Bg4 {Occasionally, Black develops the bishop out to g4 and pins our knight. We should immediately attack the bishop with h3. Of course, if Black captures on f3, we should recapture with the queen.} 7.h3 7...Bh5 {Black maintains the pin by retreating the bishop to h5.} 8.Nc3 {We develop our knight to c3. White already has a comfortable advantage and Black is the one that has to continue accurately. We will look to double Black's pawns with Bxc6, and g4 is always on the cards. Castling is flexible - we can castle kingside, but Qe2 and O-O-O is also a very reasonable option. If Black now captures on c3, we should make sure to throw in Bxc6+!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's d-Pawn Setups (2...d6/d5) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/42278661-a0a0-4bb1-b29a-f799206ccb6d/studies/42c720b4-61a3-43c6-b736-bded75ab4d3f/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5, the Sicilian Defense. We're going to play 2.b3. This is a more positional approach which looks to take our opponents into non-theoretical waters that they are probably unfamiliar with. The main idea of b3 is to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 2.b3 2...d6 {Black plays d6, supporting the c5 pawn and opening up the c8-bishop. Often 2...d6 transposes back to other structures because Black doesn't really have any other options. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight, immediately attacking the e4-pawn. Notable is that ...Nc6 would simply transpose back to the previous chapter. We'll develop our bishop to b5 and give a check. We're going for a positional game with attacking possibilities. The key is trading off the bishop before we play d3.} 4.Bb5+ 4...Nbd7 {Black's other option is to block the check by moving the b8-knight to d7. We'll support the e-pawn with d3.} 5.d3 5...a6 {Black attacks our bishop with a6. We'll capture the knight on d7.} 6.Bxd7+ 6...Bxd7 {Black recaptures with the bishop. We'll expand with f4.} 7.f4 7...e6 {Black plays e6, preparing to develop the dark-squared bishop and supporting a future d5. We'll develop our queenside knight.} 8.Nd2 8...Be7 {Black develops the bishop to e7, preparing to castle kingside.} 9.g4 {We lash out with g4! The computer might only give White a slight edge, but Black has to play accurately in order to parry the attack that is coming their way. Some of our attacking ideas include g5, h4 and Qf3.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's d-Pawn Setups (2...d6/d5) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/42278661-a0a0-4bb1-b29a-f799206ccb6d/studies/42c720b4-61a3-43c6-b736-bded75ab4d3f/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5, the Sicilian Defense. We're going to play 2.b3. This is a more positional approach which looks to take our opponents into non-theoretical waters that they are probably unfamiliar with. The main idea of b3 is to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 2.b3 2...d6 {Black plays d6, supporting the c5 pawn and opening up the c8-bishop. Often 2...d6 transposes back to other structures because Black doesn't really have any other options. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight, immediately attacking the e4-pawn. Notable is that ...Nc6 would simply transpose back to the previous chapter. We'll develop our bishop to b5 and give a check. We're going for a positional game with attacking possibilities. The key is trading off the bishop before we play d3.} 4.Bb5+ 4...Nbd7 {Black's other option is to block the check by moving the b8-knight to d7. We'll support the e-pawn with d3.} 5.d3 5...a6 {Black attacks our bishop with a6. We'll capture the knight on d7.} 6.Bxd7+ 6...Bxd7 {Black recaptures with the bishop. We'll expand with f4.} 7.f4 7...g6 {Black plays g6, preparing to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop. We'll develop our knight to f3, preparing to castle kingside.} 8.Nf3 8...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We'll castle into safety.} 9.O-O 9...O-O {Black castles.} 10.Qe2 {We develop our queen to e2, connecting the rooks and further supporting the e4 and c4 squares. White now has a slight edge, and we should look to continue with Nbd2.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's d-Pawn Setups (2...d6/d5) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/42278661-a0a0-4bb1-b29a-f799206ccb6d/studies/42c720b4-61a3-43c6-b736-bded75ab4d3f/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5, the Sicilian Defense. We're going to play 2.b3. This is a more positional approach which looks to take our opponents into non-theoretical waters that they are probably unfamiliar with. The main idea of b3 is to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 2.b3 2...d6 {Black plays d6, supporting the c5 pawn and opening up the c8-bishop. Often 2...d6 transposes back to other structures because Black doesn't really have any other options. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight, immediately attacking the e4-pawn. Notable is that ...Nc6 would simply transpose back to the previous chapter. We'll develop our bishop to b5 and give a check. We're going for a positional game with attacking possibilities. The key is trading off the bishop before we play d3.} 4.Bb5+ 4...Bd7 {Most often, Black blocks the check by moving the light-squared bishop to d7. Blocking the check with ...Nc6 would transpose back to the previous chapter. After Bd7 we'll trade the bishops.} 5.Bxd7+ 5...Nbxd7 {Black recaptures with the knight. We'll support the e-pawn with d3.} 6.d3 6...g6 {Black plays g6, preparing to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop. We'll expand with f4.} 7.f4 7...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We'll develop our knight to f3, preparing to castle kingside.} 8.Nf3 8...O-O {Black castles their king. We'll do likewise.} 9.O-O 9...b5 {Black expands on the queenside. The alternative expansion of d5 should be met with e5!} 10.Qe2 {We develop our queen to e2, connecting the rooks and further supporting the e4 and c4 squares. White now has a slight edge, and we should look to continue with Nbd2. If Black expands with a5, we should meet it with a4.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's d-Pawn Setups (2...d6/d5) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/42278661-a0a0-4bb1-b29a-f799206ccb6d/studies/42c720b4-61a3-43c6-b736-bded75ab4d3f/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5, the Sicilian Defense. We're going to play 2.b3. This is a more positional approach which looks to take our opponents into non-theoretical waters that they are probably unfamiliar with. The main idea of b3 is to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 2.b3 2...d6 {Black plays d6, supporting the c5 pawn and opening up the c8-bishop. Often 2...d6 transposes back to other structures because Black doesn't really have any other options. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight, immediately attacking the e4-pawn. Notable is that ...Nc6 would simply transpose back to the previous chapter. We'll develop our bishop to b5 and give a check. We're going for a positional game with attacking possibilities. The key is trading off the bishop before we play d3.} 4.Bb5+ 4...Bd7 {Most often, Black blocks the check by moving the light-squared bishop to d7. Blocking the check with ...Nc6 would transpose back to the previous chapter. After Bd7 we'll trade the bishops.} 5.Bxd7+ 5...Nbxd7 {Black recaptures with the knight. We'll support the e-pawn with d3.} 6.d3 6...e5 {Black might also play e5, taking central space and increasing control over the d4 square. We'll develop our kingside knight to e2 so as to keep the f4 push available.} 7.Ne2 7...Be7 {Black develops the dark-squared bishop, preparing O-O. We'll castle our king.} 8.O-O 8...O-O {Black castles their king.} 9.Nbc3 {We develop our queenside knight. White now has a comfortable edge, and we'll look to proceed with f4. Any intended expansion from Black with a6 should be met with a4.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's d-Pawn Setups (2...d6/d5) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/42278661-a0a0-4bb1-b29a-f799206ccb6d/studies/42c720b4-61a3-43c6-b736-bded75ab4d3f/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5, the Sicilian Defense. We're going to play 2.b3. This is a more positional approach which looks to take our opponents into non-theoretical waters that they are probably unfamiliar with. The main idea of b3 is to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 2.b3 2...d6 {Black plays d6, supporting the c5 pawn and opening up the c8-bishop. Often 2...d6 transposes back to other structures because Black doesn't really have any other options. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight, immediately attacking the e4-pawn. Notable is that ...Nc6 would simply transpose back to the previous chapter. We'll develop our bishop to b5 and give a check. We're going for a positional game with attacking possibilities. The key is trading off the bishop before we play d3.} 4.Bb5+ 4...Bd7 {Most often, Black blocks the check by moving the light-squared bishop to d7. Blocking the check with ...Nc6 would transpose back to the previous chapter. After Bd7 we'll trade the bishops.} 5.Bxd7+ 5...Nbxd7 {Black recaptures with the knight. We'll support the e-pawn with d3.} 6.d3 6...e6 {Black plays e6, preparing to develop the dark-squared bishop and supporting a future d5. We'll expand with f4.} 7.f4 7...Be7 {Black develops the bishop to e7, preparing to castle kingside. If Black tries d5, we should push forward with e5.} 8.g4 {We lash out with g4! The computer might only give White a slight edge, but Black has to play like a "machine" in order to parry the intimidating attack that is coming their way. Some of our attacking ideas include g5, h4 and Qf3. Our queenside knight can also develop to c3.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's d-Pawn Setups (2...d6/d5) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/42278661-a0a0-4bb1-b29a-f799206ccb6d/studies/42c720b4-61a3-43c6-b736-bded75ab4d3f/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5, the Sicilian Defense. We're going to play 2.b3. This is a more positional approach which looks to take our opponents into non-theoretical waters that they are probably unfamiliar with. The main idea of b3 is to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 2.b3 2...d6 {Black plays d6, supporting the c5 pawn and opening up the c8-bishop. Often 2...d6 transposes back to other structures because Black doesn't really have any other options. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight, immediately attacking the e4-pawn. Notable is that ...Nc6 would simply transpose back to the previous chapter. We'll develop our bishop to b5 and give a check. We're going for a positional game with attacking possibilities. The key is trading off the bishop before we play d3.} 4.Bb5+ 4...Bd7 {Most often, Black blocks the check by moving the light-squared bishop to d7. Blocking the check with ...Nc6 would transpose back to the previous chapter. After Bd7 we'll trade the bishops.} 5.Bxd7+ 5...Qxd7 {Black recaptures with the queen - this is the most common. We'll support the e-pawn with d3.} 6.d3 6...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight. g6 is also playable, but will quickly transpose. We'll expand with f4.} 7.f4 7...e6 {Black plays e6, preparing to develop the dark-squared bishop and supporting a future d5. If an immediate d5 here, it is important that we support the e-pawn with Nbd2! We'll develop our kingside knight to f3, preparing to castle kingside.} 8.Nf3 8...Be7 {Black develops the bishop to e7, preparing to castle kingside. We'll castle our king.} 9.O-O 9...O-O {Black castles their king. If Black instead strikes in the center with d5, we should look to meet it with Ne5 (see Gelashvili - Amanov, 2006).} 10.c4 {We expand with c4, controlling the important b5 and d5 squares. Similarly to the position with g6 and Bg7, the computer gives equality, but the position remains incredibly rich. We'll look to continue with Nc3 and Qe2.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's d-Pawn Setups (2...d6/d5) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...d6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/42278661-a0a0-4bb1-b29a-f799206ccb6d/studies/42c720b4-61a3-43c6-b736-bded75ab4d3f/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5, the Sicilian Defense. We're going to play 2.b3. This is a more positional approach which looks to take our opponents into non-theoretical waters that they are probably unfamiliar with. The main idea of b3 is to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 2.b3 2...d6 {Black plays d6, supporting the c5 pawn and opening up the c8-bishop. Often 2...d6 transposes back to other structures because Black doesn't really have any other options. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight, immediately attacking the e4-pawn. Notable is that ...Nc6 would simply transpose back to the previous chapter. We'll develop our bishop to b5 and give a check. We're going for a positional game with attacking possibilities. The key is trading off the bishop before we play d3.} 4.Bb5+ 4...Bd7 {Most often, Black blocks the check by moving the light-squared bishop to d7. Blocking the check with ...Nc6 would transpose back to the previous chapter. After Bd7 we'll trade the bishops.} 5.Bxd7+ 5...Qxd7 {Black recaptures with the queen - this is the most common. We'll support the e-pawn with d3.} 6.d3 6...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight. g6 is also playable, but will quickly transpose. We'll expand with f4.} 7.f4 7...g6 {Black plays g6, preparing to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop. We'll develop our kingside knight to f3, preparing to castle kingside.} 8.Nf3 8...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We'll castle our king.} 9.O-O 9...O-O {Black castles their king.} 10.c4 {We expand with c4, controlling the important b5 and d5 squares. The computer gives equality, but the position is incredibly rich. We'll look to continue with Nc3 and Qd2. To gain a better understanding of these positions, check out the game Gelashvili - Pambalos, 2018.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's d-Pawn Setups (2...d6/d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...d5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/42278661-a0a0-4bb1-b29a-f799206ccb6d/studies/e6f92685-4a50-4b22-a4cf-f1f93a693fe8/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. This is the starting position of the Sicilian Defense. We'll play b3, preparing to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop.} 2.b3 2...d5 {Every now and then, Black chooses to strike in the center with d5. We'll proceed by capturing on d5.} 3.exd5 3...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5. We'll develop our knight to c3 and attack Black's queen.} 4.Nc3 4...Qd8 {Most often, Black retreats the queen to its home square, d8. We'll proceed by fianchettoing the dark-squared bishop, as planned.} 5.Bb2 5...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight. We'll develop our kingside knight to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...Nc6 {Black develops their other knight. We'll develop the light-squared bishop to b5, threatening to capture on c6 and double Black's c-pawns.} 7.Bb5 7...Bd7 {Black develops the kingside knight, transposing back into the main line.} 8.Qe2 {We move our queen to e2, preparing to castle queenside. The superior piece activity and lead in development secure White a comfortable edge. As mentioned, we will play O-O-O and can look to play for Bxc6 and d2-d4.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's d-Pawn Setups (2...d6/d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...d5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/42278661-a0a0-4bb1-b29a-f799206ccb6d/studies/e6f92685-4a50-4b22-a4cf-f1f93a693fe8/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. This is the starting position of the Sicilian Defense. We'll play b3, preparing to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop.} 2.b3 2...d5 {Every now and then, Black chooses to strike in the center with d5. We'll proceed by capturing on d5.} 3.exd5 3...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5. We'll develop our knight to c3 and attack Black's queen.} 4.Nc3 4...Qd8 {Most often, Black retreats the queen to its home square, d8. We'll proceed by fianchettoing the dark-squared bishop, as planned.} 5.Bb2 5...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight. We'll develop the light-squared bishop to b5, threatening to capture on c6 and double Black's c-pawns.} 6.Bb5 6...Bd7 {Black develops the bishop to d7, supporting the c6 knight. We'll develop our kingside knight to f3.} 7.Nf3 7...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight, transposing back into the main line.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's d-Pawn Setups (2...d6/d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...d5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/42278661-a0a0-4bb1-b29a-f799206ccb6d/studies/e6f92685-4a50-4b22-a4cf-f1f93a693fe8/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. This is the starting position of the Sicilian Defense. We'll play b3, preparing to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop.} 2.b3 2...d5 {Every now and then, Black chooses to strike in the center with d5. We'll proceed by capturing on d5.} 3.exd5 3...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5. We'll develop our knight to c3 and attack Black's queen.} 4.Nc3 4...Qe5+ {Black moves the queen to e5 and gives a check to our king. We'll block the check with our bishop. In a perfect world, we intend to follow up with Bb2, Nf3 and O-O.} 5.Be2 5...Bg4 {Black makes use of the pin by moving the bishop out to g4. We can't now play Nf3, because Bxf3 would shatter our pawn structure due to the pin. Therefore, we'll attack Black's queen with f4!} 6.f4 6...Qe6 {Most often, Black responds by sliding the queen back to e6. We'll proceed by fianchettoing the dark-squared bishop.} 7.Bb2 7...Nc6 {Black develops the queenside knight.} 8.d3 {We proceed with d3. White now has a pleasant edge, and we should look to continue with Qd2 and O-O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's d-Pawn Setups (2...d6/d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...d5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/42278661-a0a0-4bb1-b29a-f799206ccb6d/studies/e6f92685-4a50-4b22-a4cf-f1f93a693fe8/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. This is the starting position of the Sicilian Defense. We'll play b3, preparing to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop.} 2.b3 2...d5 {Every now and then, Black chooses to strike in the center with d5. We'll proceed by capturing on d5.} 3.exd5 3...Nf6 {Some players try to avoid losing the lead in development by attacking the pawn with 3...Nf6. We'll quickly develop our bishop to b5 and give a check.} 4.Bb5+ 4...Bd7 {Black blocks the check by developing the bishop to d7. If Black instead blocks with the knight, we should support the d-pawn with c4. After Bd7, we'll simply exchange bishops.} 5.Bxd7+ 5...Qxd7 {Black recaptures with the queen. We'll defend our d-pawn with c4!} 6.c4 6...e6 {Black plays e6, looking to chip away at our central pawn wedge. We'll respond by capturing on e6.} 7.dxe6 7...Qxe6+ {Black recaptures with the queen, giving a check to our king.} 8.Qe2 {We block the check with our queen. With the extra pawn, we now have a clear advantage, and should look to continue with Bb2 and Nc3.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's d-Pawn Setups (2...d6/d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...d5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/42278661-a0a0-4bb1-b29a-f799206ccb6d/studies/e6f92685-4a50-4b22-a4cf-f1f93a693fe8/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. This is the starting position of the Sicilian Defense. We'll play b3, preparing to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop.} 2.b3 2...d5 {Every now and then, Black chooses to strike in the center with d5. We'll proceed by capturing on d5.} 3.exd5 3...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5. We'll develop our knight to c3 and attack Black's queen.} 4.Nc3 4...Qe5+ {Black moves the queen to e5 and gives a check to our king. We'll block the check with our bishop. In a perfect world, we intend to follow up with Bb2, Nf3 and O-O.} 5.Be2 5...Bg4 {Black makes use of the pin by moving the bishop out to g4. We can't now play Nf3, because Bxf3 would shatter our pawn structure due to the pin. Therefore, we'll attack Black's queen with f4!} 6.f4 6...Bxe2 {Black captures the bishop on e2. We'll recapture with the g-knight.} 7.Ngxe2 7...Qc7 {Black retreats the queen to c7.} 8.Bb2 {We develop our bishop to b2. White now has a considerable lead in development, thanks to the time Black has spent on the queen's adventures, and we'll look to continue with d3, Qd2 and O-O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's d-Pawn Setups (2...d6/d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...d5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/42278661-a0a0-4bb1-b29a-f799206ccb6d/studies/e6f92685-4a50-4b22-a4cf-f1f93a693fe8/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. This is the starting position of the Sicilian Defense. We'll play b3, preparing to fianchetto our dark-squared bishop.} 2.b3 2...d5 {Every now and then, Black chooses to strike in the center with d5. We'll proceed by capturing on d5.} 3.exd5 3...Qxd5 {Black recaptures on d5. We'll develop our knight to c3 and attack Black's queen.} 4.Nc3 4...Qd8 {Most often, Black retreats the queen to its home square, d8. We'll proceed by fianchettoing the dark-squared bishop, as planned.} 5.Bb2 5...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight. We'll develop our kingside knight to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...e6 {Black opens up the dark-squared bishop with e6. If instead g6, we should make use of the a1-h8 diagonal by jumping our knight into e4, exploiting the pin.} 7.Qe2 {We move our queen to e2, preparing to castle queenside. White simply has a very comfortable advantage and great attacking prospects with g4. The game Bauer - Wirig, 2005. perfectly displays how quickly and easily Black might get crushed from this position.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's Other Options On Move 2 (2...e6/g6/Nf6) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...e6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/0f92d237-677d-4aa4-a066-dd462e650d62/studies/b5b7e560-1e30-4836-9c4b-81fefbf68234/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll go for 2.b3.} 2.b3 2...e6 {Black plays e6 on move two. With this move, Black has opened up the dark-squared bishop and is preparing a future central expansion with d5. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move, e6, by expanding in the center with d5. Note that playing ...Nc6 here instead would transpose to Chapter 1, Study 3. After Black's move of d5, we'll proceed by capturing the pawn.} 4.exd5 4...exd5 {Black recaptures with the e-pawn. We develop our bishop to b5 and give a check to Black's king.} 5.Bb5+ 5...Nc6 {Black blocks the check by developing their queenside knight to c6. We'll develop our kingside knight to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight. We'll move our queen to e2 and give a check to Black's king. Black now has three ways to block the check, and all of them have their downsides.} 7.Qe2+ 7...Be6 {Black blocks the check by moving their c8-bishop to e6 - this move is second in popularity. We'll jump our knight into g5, attacking Black's bishop and threatening to win a pawn!} 8.Ng5 8...Qd7 {Black supports the bishop by moving the queen to d7. We'll capture Black's bishop on e6.} 9.Nxe6 9...fxe6 {Black recaptures with the f-pawn. If they instead recapture with the queen, we can capture on f6 and double their f-pawns! We'll castle our king.} 10.O-O 10...O-O-O {Black castles queenside.} 11.d3 {We continue with d3, looking to develop our knight to d2. The position is very rich and White has a promising edge. Long-term, we can look to put pressure on Black's e6-pawn and make use of the weak e5 square.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's Other Options On Move 2 (2...e6/g6/Nf6) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...e6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/0f92d237-677d-4aa4-a066-dd462e650d62/studies/b5b7e560-1e30-4836-9c4b-81fefbf68234/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll go for 2.b3.} 2.b3 2...e6 {Black plays e6 on move two. With this move, Black has opened up the dark-squared bishop and is preparing a future central expansion with d5. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move, e6, by expanding in the center with d5. Note that playing ...Nc6 here instead would transpose to Chapter 1, Study 3. After Black's move of d5, we'll proceed by capturing the pawn.} 4.exd5 4...exd5 {Black recaptures with the e-pawn. We develop our bishop to b5 and give a check to Black's king.} 5.Bb5+ 5...Nc6 {Black blocks the check by developing their queenside knight to c6. We'll develop our kingside knight to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight. We'll move our queen to e2 and give a check to Black's king. Black now has three ways to block the check, and all of them have their downsides.} 7.Qe2+ 7...Qe7 {Black blocks the check by moving their queen to e7. We'll jump our knight into e5, adding an attacker to Black's c6-knight!} 8.Ne5 8...Bd7 {Black develops the light-squared bishop and supports the c6-knight. We'll capture on c6 with our bishop.} 9.Bxc6 9...Bxc6 {Black recaptures on c6. We'll capture Black's bishop, forcing Black to double their c-pawns!} 10.Nxc6 10...bxc6 {Black recaptures on c6.} 11.Bxf6 {As if the doubled pawns on the c-file weren't enough, we now give Black doubled pawns on the f-file by capturing the knight on f6! The arising position is very pleasant, and Black's abhorrent structure simply secures White a lasting advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's Other Options On Move 2 (2...e6/g6/Nf6) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...e6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/0f92d237-677d-4aa4-a066-dd462e650d62/studies/b5b7e560-1e30-4836-9c4b-81fefbf68234/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll go for 2.b3.} 2.b3 2...e6 {Black plays e6 on move two. With this move, Black has opened up the dark-squared bishop and is preparing a future central expansion with d5. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move, e6, by expanding in the center with d5. Note that playing ...Nc6 here instead would transpose to Chapter 1, Study 3. After Black's move of d5, we'll proceed by capturing the pawn.} 4.exd5 4...exd5 {Black recaptures with the e-pawn. We develop our bishop to b5 and give a check to Black's king.} 5.Bb5+ 5...Nc6 {Black blocks the check by developing their queenside knight to c6. We'll develop our kingside knight to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...Qe7+ {Black moves the queen to e7 and gives a check to our king. This is the only serious alternative to 6...Nf6.} 7.Be2 {We block the check by retreating our bishop to e2. The position is now roughly equal, and we should look to continue with O-O and d4.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's Other Options On Move 2 (2...e6/g6/Nf6) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...e6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/0f92d237-677d-4aa4-a066-dd462e650d62/studies/b5b7e560-1e30-4836-9c4b-81fefbf68234/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll go for 2.b3.} 2.b3 2...e6 {Black plays e6 on move two. With this move, Black has opened up the dark-squared bishop and is preparing a future central expansion with d5. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move, e6, by expanding in the center with d5. Note that playing ...Nc6 here instead would transpose to Chapter 1, Study 3. After Black's move of d5, we'll proceed by capturing the pawn.} 4.exd5 4...exd5 {Black recaptures with the e-pawn. We develop our bishop to b5 and give a check to Black's king.} 5.Bb5+ 5...Nc6 {Black blocks the check by developing their queenside knight to c6. We'll develop our kingside knight to f3.} 6.Nf3 6...Nf6 {Black develops their kingside knight. We'll move our queen to e2 and give a check to Black's king. Black now has three ways to block the check, and all of them have their downsides.} 7.Qe2+ 7...Be7 {Most often, Black blocks the check by moving their f8-bishop to e7. Oops! We'll capture on f6, forcing Black to double their f-pawns.} 8.Bxf6 8...gxf6 {Black recaptures on f6.} 9.O-O {We castle our king. Due to Black's ruined structure, White now has a very comfortable edge, and should look to continue with d4 and Nc3.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's Other Options On Move 2 (2...e6/g6/Nf6) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...e6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/0f92d237-677d-4aa4-a066-dd462e650d62/studies/b5b7e560-1e30-4836-9c4b-81fefbf68234/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll go for 2.b3.} 2.b3 2...e6 {Black plays e6 on move two. With this move, Black has opened up the dark-squared bishop and is preparing a future central expansion with d5. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move, e6, by expanding in the center with d5. Note that playing ...Nc6 here instead would transpose to Chapter 1, Study 3. After Black's move of d5, we'll proceed by capturing the pawn.} 4.exd5 4...exd5 {Black recaptures with the e-pawn. We develop our bishop to b5 and give a check to Black's king.} 5.Bb5+ 5...Bd7 {Black can also block the check by moving the bishop to d7. We'll move our queen to e2 and give a check to Black's king.} 6.Qe2+ 6...Qe7 {Black blocks the check by moving their queen to e7 (note that blocking with the bishop loses immediately to Bxg7). We'll proceed by capturing on d7.} 7.Bxd7+ 7...Nxd7 {Black recaptures with the knight.} 8.d4 {We expand in the center with d4. The arising position is probably close to equality, but the computer gives White a slight edge. If Black now trades queens, we should recapture with the knight and castle kingside on the next move.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's Other Options On Move 2 (2...e6/g6/Nf6) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...e6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/0f92d237-677d-4aa4-a066-dd462e650d62/studies/b5b7e560-1e30-4836-9c4b-81fefbf68234/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll go for 2.b3.} 2.b3 2...e6 {Black plays e6 on move two. With this move, Black has opened up the dark-squared bishop and is preparing a future central expansion with d5. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...d5 {Black follows up on their previous move, e6, by expanding in the center with d5. Note that playing ...Nc6 here instead would transpose to Chapter 1, Study 3. After Black's move of d5, we'll proceed by capturing the pawn.} 4.exd5 4...exd5 {Black recaptures with the e-pawn. We develop our bishop to b5 and give a check to Black's king.} 5.Bb5+ 5...Bd7 {Black can also block the check by moving the bishop to d7. We'll move our queen to e2 and give a check to Black's king.} 6.Qe2+ 6...Ne7 {Blocking with the knight is simply too passive. Against this, we can simply proceed with natural development (as highlighted in green).} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's Other Options On Move 2 (2...e6/g6/Nf6) / Study 1: Black Plays 2...e6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/0f92d237-677d-4aa4-a066-dd462e650d62/studies/b5b7e560-1e30-4836-9c4b-81fefbf68234/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll go for 2.b3.} 2.b3 2...e6 {Black plays e6 on move two. With this move, Black has opened up the dark-squared bishop and is preparing a future central expansion with d5. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop.} 3.Bb2 3...a6 {Occasionally, Black plays a6 on move three. This early a6 is tailored to prevent us from occupying b5 at any point in the future.} 4.g3 {Against 3...a6, we should look to set up with g3, Bg2, Ne2/Nf3 and O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's Other Options On Move 2 (2...e6/g6/Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...g6 & 2...Nf6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/0f92d237-677d-4aa4-a066-dd462e650d62/studies/53d072ce-45c7-4c68-9f1b-09812ce5abe3/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll go for 2.b3.} 2.b3 2...g6 {Black plays g6 on move two. This might not look normal considering we are first to the diagonal, but it actually scores the best for Black - so let's not underestimate it. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop. We are now threatening to capture Black's rook on h8.} 3.Bb2 3...Nf6 {Black obstructs the diagonal by developing the kingside knight to f6. We'll develop our queen to f3. This interesting approach activates the queen and looks to expose the potential weakness of the f7 square.} 4.Qf3 4...d6 {Black plays d6, supporting the c5-pawn and opening up the c8-bishop. We'll develop our bishop to b5 and give a check to Black's king.} 5.Bb5+ 5...Nbd7 {Black blocks the check by moving the b8-knight to d7 - this is the critical line! We'll capture Black's knight on f6, forcing Black to double their f-pawns.} 6.Bxf6 6...exf6 {Black recaptures on f6. We'll slide our queen over to g3. The queen is well placed on g3 since it pressures the d6-pawn and ties down Black's dark-squared bishop.} 7.Qg3 7...a6 {Black attacks our bishop with a6. We'll capture the knight on d7.} 8.Bxd7+ 8...Bxd7 {Black recaptures with the bishop.} 9.Nc3 {We develop the queenside knight. This position is roughly equal, but requires practical tests. Black has the bishop pair, but is held back by a passive position and closed pawn structure. We have the d4/d5/d6 squares as a focus, with the d5 outpost for our knights and a potential pawn break (d4). We can look to continue with Nge2 and O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's Other Options On Move 2 (2...e6/g6/Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...g6 & 2...Nf6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/0f92d237-677d-4aa4-a066-dd462e650d62/studies/53d072ce-45c7-4c68-9f1b-09812ce5abe3/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll go for 2.b3.} 2.b3 2...g6 {Black plays g6 on move two. This might not look normal considering we are first to the diagonal, but it actually scores the best for Black - so let's not underestimate it. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop. We are now threatening to capture Black's rook on h8.} 3.Bb2 3...Nf6 {Black obstructs the diagonal by developing the kingside knight to f6. We'll develop our queen to f3. This interesting approach activates the queen and looks to expose the potential weakness of the f7 square.} 4.Qf3 4...d6 {Black plays d6, supporting the c5-pawn and opening up the c8-bishop. We'll develop our bishop to b5 and give a check to Black's king.} 5.Bb5+ 5...Nc6 {Occasionally, Black blocks the check by moving the b8-knight to c6. We'll push forward with e5!} 6.e5 6...dxe5 {Black captures on e5. We'll capture Black's knight, forcing them to double their c-pawns!} 7.Bxc6+ 7...bxc6 {Black recaptures on c6.} 8.Bxe5 {We recapture on e5. With the imminent threat Qxc6+, and with the positional idea Bxf6 (further ruining Black's pawn structure), White now enjoys a slight edge.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's Other Options On Move 2 (2...e6/g6/Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...g6 & 2...Nf6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/0f92d237-677d-4aa4-a066-dd462e650d62/studies/53d072ce-45c7-4c68-9f1b-09812ce5abe3/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll go for 2.b3.} 2.b3 2...g6 {Black plays g6 on move two. This might not look normal considering we are first to the diagonal, but it actually scores the best for Black - so let's not underestimate it. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop. We are now threatening to capture Black's rook on h8.} 3.Bb2 3...Nf6 {Black obstructs the diagonal by developing the kingside knight to f6. We'll develop our queen to f3. This interesting approach activates the queen and looks to expose the potential weakness of the f7 square.} 4.Qf3 4...d6 {Black plays d6, supporting the c5-pawn and opening up the c8-bishop. We'll develop our bishop to b5 and give a check to Black's king.} 5.Bb5+ 5...Bd7 {Most often, Black blocks the check by moving the light-squared bishop to d7. We'll capture Black's knight, forcing Black to double their f-pawns!} 6.Bxf6 6...exf6 {Black recaptures on f6. We'll proceed by trading bishops on d7.} 7.Bxd7+ 7...Nxd7 {Black recaptures with the knight.} 8.Nc3 {We develop the queenside knight. Due to Black's inferior structure, White now enjoys a slight, but long-term, positional edge. We should look to continue developing with Nge2 and O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's Other Options On Move 2 (2...e6/g6/Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...g6 & 2...Nf6 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/0f92d237-677d-4aa4-a066-dd462e650d62/studies/53d072ce-45c7-4c68-9f1b-09812ce5abe3/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll go for 2.b3.} 2.b3 2...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks our e-pawn. We'll push forward and attack Black's knight.} 3.e5 3...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. We'll develop the kingside knight to f3.} 4.Nf3 4...g6 {Black plays g6, looking to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop. Instead, Nc6 would transpose to Chapter 1, Study 4. We'll strike in the center with d4!} 5.d4 5...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We'll capture on c5 and open up an attack on Black's knight.} 6.dxc5 6...Qa5+ {Black tries to win the pawn back by moving the queen out to a5 and giving a check to our king, but this natural move is actually a blunder! We'll block the check with c3.} 7.c3 7...Qxc5 {Black captures the pawn on c5. We'll push the b-pawn and attack Black's queen!} 8.b4 8...Qc6 {Black retreats the queen to c6 - still defending the d5-knight.} 9.b5 {We push the b-pawn once more, and Black has no way to save the knight! Qe6 now loses the knight to Bc4... And Qc5 loses the knight to Ba3!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's Other Options On Move 2 (2...e6/g6/Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...g6 & 2...Nf6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/0f92d237-677d-4aa4-a066-dd462e650d62/studies/53d072ce-45c7-4c68-9f1b-09812ce5abe3/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll go for 2.b3.} 2.b3 2...g6 {Black plays g6 on move two. This might not look normal considering we are first to the diagonal, but it actually scores the best for Black - so let's not underestimate it. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop. We are now threatening to capture Black's rook on h8.} 3.Bb2 3...Nf6 {Black obstructs the diagonal by developing the kingside knight to f6. We'll develop our queen to f3. This interesting approach activates the queen and looks to expose the potential weakness of the f7 square.} 4.Qf3 4...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes the dark-squared bishop. This is, however, slightly inaccurate. We'll push our e-pawn and attack Black's knight!} 5.e5 5...Ng8 {Black retreats the knight to its home square, g8. It couldn't have gone to h5, because then g4 would trap and win it. Now e6 both opens up an attack on the bishop and threatens checkmate!} 6.e6 6...Nf6 {Black blocks both threats by jumping the knight back to f6. We'll proceed by capturing on f7.} 7.exf7+ 7...Kxf7 {Black recaptures on f7. We'll push forward with g4! This move threatens to win Black's pinned knight with g5.} 8.g4 8...h6 {Black plays h6, preventing g5.} 9.h4 {We push h4, further supporting g5! The computer evaluates this position as slightly better for White. From a human standpoint, this is a difficult position to navigate as Black. Black's misplaced king, in conjunction with the raging kingside pawn storm, should give White good practical chances.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's Other Options On Move 2 (2...e6/g6/Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...g6 & 2...Nf6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/0f92d237-677d-4aa4-a066-dd462e650d62/studies/53d072ce-45c7-4c68-9f1b-09812ce5abe3/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll go for 2.b3.} 2.b3 2...g6 {Black plays g6 on move two. This might not look normal considering we are first to the diagonal, but it actually scores the best for Black - so let's not underestimate it. We'll follow up on our previous move by fianchettoing the c1-bishop. We are now threatening to capture Black's rook on h8.} 3.Bb2 3...Nf6 {Black obstructs the diagonal by developing the kingside knight to f6. We'll develop our queen to f3. This interesting approach activates the queen and looks to expose the potential weakness of the f7 square.} 4.Qf3 4...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes the dark-squared bishop. This is, however, slightly inaccurate. We'll push our e-pawn and attack Black's knight!} 5.e5 5...Ng8 {Black retreats the knight to its home square, g8. It couldn't have gone to h5, because then g4 would trap and win it. Now e6 both opens up an attack on the bishop and threatens checkmate!} 6.e6 6...f6 {Black blocks both threats by pushing the f-pawn one square.} 7.exd7+ {We'll respond by capturing on d7. Regardless of how Black recaptures, White should be able to secure a comfortable edge with Nc3 and O-O-O. Of course, recapturing with the bishop would simply hang the b7-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's Other Options On Move 2 (2...e6/g6/Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...g6 & 2...Nf6 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/0f92d237-677d-4aa4-a066-dd462e650d62/studies/53d072ce-45c7-4c68-9f1b-09812ce5abe3/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll go for 2.b3.} 2.b3 2...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks our e-pawn. We'll push forward and attack Black's knight.} 3.e5 3...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. We'll develop the kingside knight to f3.} 4.Nf3 4...g6 {Black plays g6, looking to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop. Instead, Nc6 would transpose to Chapter 1, Study 4. We'll strike in the center with d4!} 5.d4 5...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop. We'll capture on c5 and open up an attack on Black's knight.} 6.dxc5 6...Nc7 {Black retreats the knight to c7.} 7.Bb2 {We fianchetto the c1-bishop. White is now simply up a pawn and Black has little to no compensation for it. We should look to continue with Nc3, Qd2 and O-O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's Other Options On Move 2 (2...e6/g6/Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...g6 & 2...Nf6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/0f92d237-677d-4aa4-a066-dd462e650d62/studies/53d072ce-45c7-4c68-9f1b-09812ce5abe3/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll go for 2.b3.} 2.b3 2...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks our e-pawn. We'll push forward and attack Black's knight.} 3.e5 3...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. We'll develop the kingside knight to f3.} 4.Nf3 4...g6 {Black plays g6, looking to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop. Instead, Nc6 would transpose to Chapter 1, Study 4. We'll strike in the center with d4!} 5.d4 5...cxd4 {Black captures the pawn on d4. We'll recapture on d4 and attack Black's knight.} 6.Qxd4 6...Nc7 {Black retreats the knight to c7. We'll fianchetto the dark-squared bishop.} 7.Bb2 7...Bg7 {Black fianchettoes their bishop.} 8.Nc3 {We develop our knight to c3. We now have a pleasant edge, and should look to continue with Qe3 and O-O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Black's Other Options On Move 2 (2...e6/g6/Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 2...g6 & 2...Nf6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fb973fb9-8cb3-4ac4-9ed0-42f882cf9a2b/chapters/0f92d237-677d-4aa4-a066-dd462e650d62/studies/53d072ce-45c7-4c68-9f1b-09812ce5abe3/lines"]

1.e4 1...c5 {Black replies with 1...c5. We'll go for 2.b3.} 2.b3 2...Nf6 {Black develops the kingside knight and attacks our e-pawn. We'll push forward and attack Black's knight.} 3.e5 3...Nd5 {Black jumps the knight into d5. We'll develop the kingside knight to f3.} 4.Nf3 4...g6 {Black plays g6, looking to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop. Instead, Nc6 would transpose to Chapter 1, Study 4. We'll strike in the center with d4!} 5.d4 5...cxd4 {Black captures the pawn on d4. We'll recapture on d4 and attack Black's knight.} 6.Qxd4 6...e6 {Black plays e6, defending the knight and opening up the f8-bishop.} 7.Bg5 {We expose the weakened dark squares by moving our bishop to g5. White now has a very comfortable position, which the computer evaluates as borderline winning. If Black now attacks our queen with Nc6, we should retreat it to b2. And if Black gives a check with Qa5, we should retreat our bishop to d2.} *